---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Kicker from '../../components/content/Kicker.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/convert/EmailCapture.astro';

const title = 'The Evidence Ledger Template | PainToBuild';
const description = 'The free column structure PainToBuild uses to log evidence, sources and independence before publishing any analysis — with one filled example row.';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: 'The Evidence Ledger Template',
  description,
  author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PainToBuild' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} ogImagePath="/open-graph/tools-evidence-ledger-template.png">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />

  <section class="wrap section prose">
    <Kicker>Free template</Kicker>
    <h1>The evidence ledger template</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      This is the exact row structure behind every analysis on this site — the same one used to
      decide whether a candidate has three independent signals before anything gets published.
    </p>

    <h2>What an evidence ledger is</h2>
    <p>
      A flat log, one row per piece of evidence, that forces three questions before you count
      anything toward validation: is this a distinct person, is it their own first-person words,
      and can you point at the source. Most idea-validation failures aren't a bad idea — they're
      mistaking three loud comments in one thread for three independent buyers.
    </p>

    <h2>The column structure</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Evidence ID</th><th>Candidate ID</th><th>Source platform</th><th>Distinct user?</th>
            <th>Date</th><th>Snippet / quote</th><th>Counts as independent?</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>E-001</td>
            <td>C-001</td>
            <td>Reddit, r/indiehackers</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>2026-07-16</td>
            <td>"…shutting the product down; every attempt felt like posting into the void"</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      That's a real, published row — see the full writeup in
      <a href="/evidence/community-access-is-not-buyer-access/">community access vs. buyer access</a>.
      The "distinct user?" and "counts as independent?" columns are what actually gate a candidate:
      a repost, an advisor comment, or a promotional post gets logged but marked "No," and doesn't
      count toward the three-signal threshold. Full rules on the
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Get the template</h2>
    <p>Enter your email and the full template — with the complete column set and instructions — is sent to you directly.</p>
    <EmailCapture pagePath="/tools/evidence-ledger-template/" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .section { padding-block: var(--s7) var(--s8); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--t-lede); color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; margin: var(--s5) 0; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--t-body-s); min-width: 640px; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: var(--s3); border: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top; }
  th { background: var(--surface-sunken); white-space: nowrap; }
  .note { color: var(--muted); font-size: var(--t-body-s); background: var(--surface-sunken); border-radius: var(--r-card); padding: var(--s5); }
</style>
